{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794666288,"nonce":"n71wABZvCiJuAOyMF9vLbWLZmAI=","message":"Compilation server and the exporter, measured on the stock unit suite (8902 functions).\n\nTyping through `haxe --server-listen`/`--connect` goes from 2.48 s cold to about 0.65 s warm for a stock compile. The exporter under the server cannot be trusted warm yet:\n\n- First compile through a fresh server: identical to a non-server export, apart from the build-timestamp constant the suite embeds.\n- Second compile on the same server: three differences. The compiler renames `@:native` enum constructors in place after `onGenerate`, and a cached module keeps the new names (Issue9806 `One` reads `Two`, Issue9812 `Baz` reads `BAR`). One function, `_A.A_Impl_#s:func`, is not exported at all. I did not find why.\n- Before this was understood, the unbound-monomorph difference (`?` vs `Dynamic`) accounted for 49 other differences. The exporter now writes an unbound monomorph as Dynamic, which is what genhl does.\n\nConsequence: Haxe 4.3.6 has no on-disk typed cache, and the server's in-memory cache carries state from earlier compiles that the exporter reads. The std layer caches our exported AST and stays the way std is cached. The compiler's own typing of std recurs each process unless a server is used; a server is only safe for export if the exporter stops reading anything the filters rewrite, or the driver restarts it per program. Not decided.","files":null}]}